    "result_text": "All 35 tests pass, including the new `Cp25Tests` (email-unique). BUILD SUCCESS.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the rule \"reject creating an owner whose lower-cased email is already used by any other owner (409)\" using the project's OfficeFloor function-injection pattern, mirroring the existing `CheckOwnerTelephoneUnique` / `DuplicateTelephoneException` design.\n\nFiles added:\n- **`function/owner/CheckOwnerEmailUnique.java`** \u2014 a pipeline step that reads the validated `OwnerFieldsDto` (email already lower-cased by `ValidateOwnerFields` \u2192 `OwnerEmail.normalize`) and compares it, lower-cased, against every existing owner's email. Skips when the request has no email; throws `DuplicateEmailException` on a collision.\n- **`escalation/DuplicateEmailException.java`** \u2014 checked exception (`extends Exception`).\n- **`escalation/DuplicateEmailExceptionHandler.java`** \u2014 responds `409 CONFLICT`.\n- **`officefloor/escalation/\u2026DuplicateEmailException.yml`** \u2014 wires the exception to its handler.\n\nFiles changed:\n- **`officefloor/rest/api/owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 inserted a `checkEmailUnique` step (with `govern: [ transaction ]`) between `checkHouseholdUnique` and `checkCityCapacity`.\n\nThe check runs after field validation/normalization (so email is already trimmed and lower-cased) and follows the same `findAll()` scan as the existing telephone/household uniqueness checks for consistency.",
